1
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:12,600
Welcome to the STAT 251 Exam 2 soccer edition.

2
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,833
You do not need to know any of these ideas beforehand.

3
00:00:15,833 --> 00:00:18,700
Imagine we are in a goalkeeper session:

4
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:22,133
I explain the idea, we practice it on the pitch,

5
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:26,467
and then we connect it to the exact calculation your professor could ask for.

6
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:28,467
The instructor’s original voter,

7
00:00:28,467 --> 00:00:33,367
GPA, and customer-arrival examples stay in the episode;

8
00:00:33,367 --> 00:00:37,200
our new supporting examples come entirely from soccer.

9
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:41,467
The professor’s Exam 2 guide narrows the scope in a useful way.

10
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:44,900
Module 4 is continuous random variables only.

11
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:47,500
Module 5 is confidence intervals.

12
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:49,900
Module 6 is hypothesis testing.

13
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:53,367
The exam gives you a z-table and a t-table,

14
00:00:53,367 --> 00:00:57,333
and the professor specifically wants you to use those tables rather than a

15
00:00:57,333 --> 00:01:00,167
calculator’s built-in normal calculation.

16
00:01:00,167 --> 00:01:03,933
So this podcast is going to teach you not just the formulas,

17
00:01:03,933 --> 00:01:09,400
but how to recognize which formula and which table belong to the problem in front of you.

18
00:01:09,433 --> 00:01:12,100
Before anything else, here is the big picture.

19
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:15,800
Statistics is usually trying to solve one of two problems.

20
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:20,067
First, we might know a probability model and want to calculate the chance of

21
00:01:20,067 --> 00:01:21,200
something happening.

22
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,000
That is mostly Module 4.

23
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,200
Second, we might have a sample and want to learn something about a much larger population.

24
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,467
That is Modules 5 and 6.

25
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:31,767
Think about soccer.

26
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:34,067
Suppose you are studying penalty kicks.

27
00:01:34,067 --> 00:01:37,867
If you already know a goalkeeper saves 20 percent of penalties,

28
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:41,533
you might ask, what is the probability of a certain outcome?

29
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:43,000
That is probability.

30
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,600
But suppose you do not know the true save percentage of all goalkeepers,

31
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:50,233
and you watch a sample of games to estimate it.

32
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:51,933
Now you are doing inference.

33
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:55,067
Confidence intervals estimate the unknown number.

34
00:01:55,067 --> 00:01:59,800
Hypothesis tests challenge a specific claim about the unknown number.

35
00:01:59,867 --> 00:02:02,767
That difference — probability versus inference

36
00:02:02,767 --> 00:02:05,600
— is the first thing I want you to keep in your head.

37
00:02:08,133 --> 00:02:11,800
Let’s define four words because they show up everywhere.

38
00:02:11,867 --> 00:02:14,833
A population is the whole group you care about.

39
00:02:14,833 --> 00:02:20,167
For example, all college goalkeepers in the country are one possible population.

40
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:25,667
Your professor also uses all engineering students when discussing GPA;

41
00:02:25,667 --> 00:02:29,333
that is the population in the original lecture example.

42
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:32,567
A sample is the smaller group you actually observe.

43
00:02:32,567 --> 00:02:37,533
Maybe you randomly collect GPA data from 114 students.

44
00:02:37,533 --> 00:02:40,967
Those 114 students are your sample.

45
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:44,467
A parameter is a number describing the population.

46
00:02:44,467 --> 00:02:48,200
The true population mean is written with the Greek letter mu.

47
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:50,967
The true population proportion is written p.

48
00:02:50,967 --> 00:02:53,100
Parameters are usually unknown.

49
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:55,667
That is why we are doing statistics.

50
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:59,167
A statistic is a number calculated from the sample.

51
00:02:59,167 --> 00:03:04,033
The sample mean is x-bar, or sometimes y-bar in your professor’s slides.

52
00:03:04,033 --> 00:03:06,167
The sample proportion is p-hat.

53
00:03:06,167 --> 00:03:08,933
The sample standard deviation is s.

54
00:03:08,967 --> 00:03:11,267
The whole point of inference is this:

55
00:03:11,267 --> 00:03:17,000
use the statistic you can calculate to learn about the parameter you cannot directly see.

56
00:03:17,033 --> 00:03:21,900
If a question says, “Thirty-two percent of the sampled students…” that is a sample

57
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:23,533
proportion, p-hat.

58
00:03:23,533 --> 00:03:27,500
If it says, “the true proportion of all students…” that is the population

59
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,233
proportion, p.

60
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:34,433
If the question says, “the average sprint time of 50 sampled goalkeepers,” that is a

61
00:03:34,433 --> 00:03:37,100
sample mean, written as x-bar.

62
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:41,467
If it says “the true average sprint time of all college goalkeepers,” that is a

63
00:03:41,467 --> 00:03:44,433
population mean, written as mu.

64
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:49,233
This distinction matters because formulas are built around the parameter you are

65
00:03:49,233 --> 00:03:51,567
trying to estimate or test.

66
00:03:54,133 --> 00:03:58,867
Module 4 on this exam is continuous random variables only.

67
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:03,767
A random variable is a numerical way to represent an uncertain outcome.

68
00:04:03,767 --> 00:04:07,933
A continuous random variable can take any value within an interval.

69
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:10,233
Soccer examples include sprint time,

70
00:04:10,233 --> 00:04:13,367
shot speed, distance covered, reaction time,

71
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:15,167
or how long until a goal.

72
00:04:15,167 --> 00:04:24,933
A value might be 0.420 seconds, 0.4201 seconds, or 0.42017 seconds.

73
00:04:24,967 --> 00:04:28,633
If a soccer sprint time can be 4.20 seconds,

74
00:04:28,633 --> 00:04:33,633
4.201 seconds, 4.2017 seconds,

75
00:04:33,633 --> 00:04:36,400
and so on, that is continuous.

76
00:04:36,433 --> 00:04:38,500
For a continuous random variable,

77
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:44,733
the most important picture is a curve called a probability density function, or PDF.

78
00:04:44,767 --> 00:04:48,500
The PDF is not the probability at one exact point.

79
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:50,133
It is a density curve.

80
00:04:50,133 --> 00:04:53,233
Probability comes from AREA under the curve.

81
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:57,867
This is a huge exam idea: for a continuous random variable,

82
00:04:57,867 --> 00:05:01,900
the probability that X equals one exact value is zero.

83
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:04,600
P of X equals exactly 5 is zero.

84
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:06,967
The useful probabilities are intervals,

85
00:05:06,967 --> 00:05:09,667
like P that X is between 4 and 6,

86
00:05:09,667 --> 00:05:12,233
or P that X is less than 5.

87
00:05:12,267 --> 00:05:16,467
The total area under a valid PDF must equal 1,

88
00:05:16,467 --> 00:05:19,267
because 1 means 100 percent probability.

89
00:05:19,267 --> 00:05:22,167
The density also cannot be negative.

90
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:28,333
If the question gives you a PDF f of x and asks for a probability between a and b,

91
00:05:28,333 --> 00:05:32,167
conceptually you integrate the PDF from a to b.

92
00:05:32,167 --> 00:05:37,300
In words: probability equals area under the density curve over the event.

93
00:05:38,167 --> 00:05:43,700
Now, there is another function called the CDF, the cumulative distribution function.

94
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:45,367
It is written F of x.

95
00:05:45,367 --> 00:05:50,567
The definition is F of x equals P of X less than or equal to x.

96
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:56,533
For continuous data, the CDF is the accumulated probability to the left.

97
00:05:56,533 --> 00:05:59,133
If X is the time until the next goal,

98
00:05:59,133 --> 00:06:03,667
F(20) means the probability the goal arrives within 20 minutes.

99
00:06:03,667 --> 00:06:06,833
This is how we translate the curve into an answer.

100
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:09,100
That gives you some useful shortcuts.

101
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:12,900
P of X greater than x equals 1 minus F of x.

102
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:18,300
P of a less than X less than or equal to b equals F of b minus F of a.

103
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:22,933
For a continuous variable, it does not matter whether the endpoint is less-than or

104
00:06:22,933 --> 00:06:28,267
less-than-or-equal, because a single exact point has probability zero.

105
00:06:29,133 --> 00:06:33,100
The mean, written as mu or E of X, is the expected center.

106
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:38,267
Variance describes spread; standard deviation is the square root of variance.

107
00:06:38,267 --> 00:06:40,467
These are not just definitions:

108
00:06:40,467 --> 00:06:46,100
the integral formulas let you calculate center and spread from a given PDF.

109
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:49,267
Variance measures spread around the mean.

110
00:06:49,267 --> 00:06:53,100
One convenient form is E of X squared minus mu squared.

111
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:56,633
Standard deviation is the square root of variance.

112
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:01,967
Imagine goalkeeper reaction times with mean 0.40 seconds.

113
00:07:01,967 --> 00:07:07,733
If the standard deviation is only 0.02 seconds, the group is consistent.

114
00:07:07,733 --> 00:07:13,633
If the standard deviation is 0.10 seconds, reaction times vary much more.

115
00:07:13,633 --> 00:07:17,433
The mean could stay exactly the same while the spread changes.

116
00:07:19,967 --> 00:07:24,067
The professor’s guide identifies three continuous distributions you need to

117
00:07:24,067 --> 00:07:28,533
recognize: Uniform, Exponential, and Normal.

118
00:07:28,567 --> 00:07:30,267
First, Uniform.

119
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:35,533
Use Uniform when every value within an interval has the same probability density.

120
00:07:35,533 --> 00:07:37,633
The graph is a flat rectangle.

121
00:07:37,633 --> 00:07:41,767
Suppose the coach randomly chooses when the shooting drill starts,

122
00:07:41,767 --> 00:07:44,533
equally across the 20 minutes after warmups.

123
00:07:44,533 --> 00:07:47,533
Let X be the delay before the drill begins.

124
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:54,100
If X is Uniform from a to b, the mean is the midpoint and the variance depends on

125
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:55,700
the square of the interval width.

126
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:57,900
In the drill example, a is zero,

127
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:02,000
b is twenty, and the average start delay is ten minutes.

128
00:08:02,867 --> 00:08:08,267
For a Uniform interval, probability is just desired interval length divided by total

129
00:08:08,267 --> 00:08:09,233
interval length.

130
00:08:09,233 --> 00:08:13,000
If the drill can start anywhere between zero and 20 minutes,

131
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,700
the chance of starting from five to ten minutes is five divided by twenty,

132
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:19,700
or 25 percent.

133
00:08:20,533 --> 00:08:26,800
Recognize Uniform from wording like “equally likely anywhere between…” or a flat density.

134
00:08:26,867 --> 00:08:28,867
Second, Exponential.

135
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:35,267
Use Exponential for time until the first event when arrivals happen at a constant average rate.

136
00:08:35,267 --> 00:08:40,500
An idealized soccer example is the time until the next goal if goals happened

137
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:43,467
independently at a constant average rate.

138
00:08:43,467 --> 00:08:48,467
Real soccer is not perfectly constant-rate, but the model helps explain the math.

139
00:08:48,467 --> 00:08:53,200
The professor’s customer-arrival example is the same type of question.

140
00:08:53,267 --> 00:08:55,800
For an Exponential random variable X,

141
00:08:55,800 --> 00:09:00,600
the PDF starts high and decreases as the waiting time increases.

142
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:04,433
The parameter lambda is an event rate measured per time unit.

143
00:09:04,433 --> 00:09:09,433
Do not confuse a rate of five per hour with a waiting time of five hours.

144
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:15,000
The mean wait and standard deviation are both one divided by lambda,

145
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,133
while the variance is one divided by lambda squared.

146
00:09:18,133 --> 00:09:23,167
For example, if an idealized match situation has a rate of two goals per hour,

147
00:09:23,167 --> 00:09:26,200
the average waiting time is half an hour.

148
00:09:27,033 --> 00:09:29,767
Here is a soccer version of that rate idea:

149
00:09:29,767 --> 00:09:33,133
suppose the model predicts two scoring events per hour.

150
00:09:33,133 --> 00:09:37,900
The expected time until the next scoring event is 1 divided by 2 hours,

151
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:39,267
or 30 minutes.

152
00:09:39,267 --> 00:09:44,567
The real match can be very different; the model expresses long-run averages.

153
00:09:44,633 --> 00:09:46,900
Keep the professor’s exact example.

154
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:51,633
The lecture says X is Exponential with rate five per hour and asks for the

155
00:09:51,633 --> 00:09:56,733
probability the first customer arrives between 15 and 60 minutes.

156
00:09:56,733 --> 00:10:01,967
Convert 15 minutes to 0.25 hours and 60 minutes to one hour,

157
00:10:01,967 --> 00:10:03,867
because lambda is per hour.

158
00:10:03,867 --> 00:10:06,500
Then subtract the two CDF values.

159
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:12,233
The same calculation could describe waiting for a soccer event at a five-per-hour rate.

160
00:10:13,067 --> 00:10:14,533
Third, Normal.

161
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:17,633
The Normal distribution is the bell-shaped curve.

162
00:10:17,633 --> 00:10:21,233
It is symmetric and unimodal, centered at mean mu,

163
00:10:21,233 --> 00:10:24,867
with spread controlled by standard deviation sigma.

164
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:29,533
You may see X distributed Normal with parameters mu and sigma squared.

165
00:10:29,533 --> 00:10:32,633
The second parameter in that notation is VARIANCE.

166
00:10:32,633 --> 00:10:37,133
The standard deviation is sigma, which is the square root of the variance.

167
00:10:37,133 --> 00:10:39,800
Always check which one a problem gives.

168
00:10:40,633 --> 00:10:45,167
The Normal distribution is important because many real measurements are roughly

169
00:10:45,167 --> 00:10:50,500
bell-shaped and because the Central Limit Theorem makes Normal models show up in

170
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:55,300
inference even when the original population is not perfectly Normal.

171
00:10:55,367 --> 00:11:02,033
The 68-95-99.7 rule says that for a roughly Normal distribution,

172
00:11:02,033 --> 00:11:07,067
about 68 percent of values are within one standard deviation of the mean,

173
00:11:07,067 --> 00:11:10,000
about 95 percent are within two,

174
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,933
and about 99.7 percent are within three.

175
00:11:13,967 --> 00:11:19,200
Soccer example: suppose goalkeeper reaction times are approximately Normal,

176
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:25,800
with mean 0.40 seconds and standard deviation 0.04 seconds.

177
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:31,900
About 68 percent should fall between 0.36 and 0.44 seconds.

178
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:38,067
About 95 percent should fall between 0.32 and 0.48 seconds.

179
00:11:38,067 --> 00:11:42,000
This is approximate, not a substitute for the z-table.

180
00:11:44,567 --> 00:11:50,133
A z-score converts a raw value into “how many standard deviations from the mean.”

181
00:11:50,200 --> 00:11:52,633
Here is the actual z-score formula.

182
00:11:52,633 --> 00:11:56,867
On paper it has a fraction: x minus the population mean,

183
00:11:56,867 --> 00:12:00,367
divided by the population standard deviation.

184
00:12:01,233 --> 00:12:06,500
If z equals positive 2, the value is two standard deviations above the mean.

185
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:13,333
If z equals negative 1.3, the value is 1.3 standard deviations below the mean.

186
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:14,500
Now the table.

187
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:20,667
This matters because the professor says the exam will provide the z-table and you must use it.

188
00:12:20,733 --> 00:12:23,733
Your z-table gives the area to the LEFT of z.

189
00:12:23,733 --> 00:12:25,800
That is the entire trick.

190
00:12:25,833 --> 00:12:29,767
Suppose the standard score is z equal to 1.20.

191
00:12:29,767 --> 00:12:34,667
In your provided table, find row 1.2 and column .00.

192
00:12:34,667 --> 00:12:37,167
The entry is 0.8849.

193
00:12:37,167 --> 00:12:42,967
That is the cumulative probability to the left of z equal to 1.20.

194
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:47,600
If the question instead asks for the area to the right of that score,

195
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:50,767
subtract 0.8849 from one.

196
00:12:50,767 --> 00:12:53,900
The result is 0.1151.

197
00:12:54,767 --> 00:13:02,700
If z equals negative 1.20, the table directly gives the left-tail area, 0.1151.

198
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:06,900
For negative scores, make sure you use the negative half of the table.

199
00:13:07,767 --> 00:13:10,467
If you need area between two z-scores,

200
00:13:10,467 --> 00:13:14,400
take the larger left-area minus the smaller left-area.

201
00:13:14,467 --> 00:13:19,000
For a two-sided outside probability beyond plus or minus some z,

202
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,133
find one tail and multiply by 2 if the Normal curve is symmetric.

203
00:13:24,167 --> 00:13:31,333
Example: for z equal to 2.00, the table gives 0.9772 to the left.

204
00:13:31,333 --> 00:13:34,533
The right-tail area is 0.0228.

205
00:13:34,533 --> 00:13:42,567
The probability of being beyond either positive 2 or negative 2 is twice that, 0.0456.

206
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:48,467
The z-table is used whenever the relevant standardized statistic follows the

207
00:13:48,467 --> 00:13:50,500
standard Normal distribution.

208
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:54,167
On this exam that includes Normal probability questions,

209
00:13:54,167 --> 00:13:56,600
confidence intervals for proportions,

210
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:59,533
and hypothesis tests for proportions.

211
00:14:02,100 --> 00:14:05,167
Now we bridge Module 4 into Module 5.

212
00:14:05,233 --> 00:14:08,400
Imagine repeating a goalkeeper reaction-time study.

213
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:11,700
Each time, randomly sample 50 goalkeepers,

214
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:13,800
compute their average reaction time,

215
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:15,300
and keep that average.

216
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:18,667
The individual athletes have their own variation,

217
00:14:18,667 --> 00:14:24,833
but the collection of sample averages has a new distribution called the sampling distribution.

218
00:14:24,867 --> 00:14:29,633
The Central Limit Theorem says that with a sufficiently large random sample,

219
00:14:29,633 --> 00:14:33,200
the distribution of the sample mean is approximately Normal.

220
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:34,800
Its center remains mu.

221
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:38,600
Its standard error is sigma divided by the square root of n.

222
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:44,000
That is why many statistics calculations use a bell curve even when individual

223
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,367
observations are not perfectly bell-shaped.

224
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:52,867
That standard deviation of a statistic is called its standard error.

225
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:59,367
This is a core idea: standard deviation describes variation among individual observations.

226
00:14:59,367 --> 00:15:05,567
Standard error describes variation among sample statistics from repeated samples.

227
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:10,600
As n gets larger, square root of n gets larger, so standard error gets smaller.

228
00:15:10,600 --> 00:15:13,833
Bigger sample means more precise estimate.

229
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:18,733
This explains why sample size matters so much in Modules 5 and 6.

230
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:27,700
A confidence interval is a range of plausible values for an unknown population parameter.

231
00:15:27,733 --> 00:15:32,667
The universal two-sided confidence interval structure is written with a point

232
00:15:32,667 --> 00:15:35,333
estimate and a margin of error.

233
00:15:36,167 --> 00:15:41,200
On paper, the margin of error is the critical value times the standard error.

234
00:15:41,200 --> 00:15:47,300
That single pattern is hiding underneath all the confidence-interval formulas in this unit.

235
00:15:48,133 --> 00:15:52,333
Margin of error equals critical value times standard error.

236
00:15:52,367 --> 00:15:56,700
So every confidence interval problem becomes three questions.

237
00:15:56,733 --> 00:15:59,500
One: what parameter am I estimating?

238
00:15:59,533 --> 00:16:04,233
Two: what is the point estimate and standard error for that parameter?

239
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:08,767
Three: do I use a z critical value or a t critical value?

240
00:16:08,833 --> 00:16:12,533
This is the decision process I want you to memorize.

241
00:16:12,567 --> 00:16:17,033
If the question asks for the percentage of goalkeepers who prefer natural grass,

242
00:16:17,033 --> 00:16:19,567
the target is one proportion p.

243
00:16:19,567 --> 00:16:21,900
Use p-hat and the z-table.

244
00:16:21,967 --> 00:16:26,233
If you compare the percentages of goalkeepers versus outfield players who prefer

245
00:16:26,233 --> 00:16:29,867
grass, the target is p-one minus p-two.

246
00:16:29,867 --> 00:16:33,100
Use the difference of sample proportions and z.

247
00:16:33,167 --> 00:16:37,033
If the question asks for the true average goalkeeper reaction time,

248
00:16:37,033 --> 00:16:38,967
the target is one mean mu.

249
00:16:38,967 --> 00:16:44,233
Use the sample mean and Student’s t with n minus one degrees of freedom.

250
00:16:44,267 --> 00:16:49,333
If you compare average reaction time for two independent goalkeeper training groups,

251
00:16:49,333 --> 00:16:51,800
the target is mu-one minus mu-two.

252
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:56,167
Use a two-sample t procedure with Welch degrees of freedom.

253
00:16:56,200 --> 00:17:00,933
If the data are paired, turn the data into one list of differences and use a

254
00:17:00,933 --> 00:17:03,967
one-sample t interval on the mean difference.

255
00:17:04,033 --> 00:17:05,933
Let’s go one by one.

256
00:17:07,967 --> 00:17:13,233
Use this when the question asks for one population percentage or proportion.

257
00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:18,400
The actual confidence interval for one population proportion is shown below.

258
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:21,967
Read the symbol p-hat as the observed fraction in the sample,

259
00:17:21,967 --> 00:17:24,233
q-hat as the opposite fraction,

260
00:17:24,233 --> 00:17:26,933
and n as the number sampled.

261
00:17:27,767 --> 00:17:30,133
Here the hat means a sample estimate,

262
00:17:30,133 --> 00:17:32,400
the star means a critical cutoff,

263
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:34,533
and n is the number of players.

264
00:17:34,533 --> 00:17:38,067
A fraction in the square root gives the standard error.

265
00:17:38,133 --> 00:17:41,700
In that formula, q-hat equals one minus p-hat.

266
00:17:41,700 --> 00:17:43,967
The square root is the standard error.

267
00:17:43,967 --> 00:17:47,567
Z-star is the critical value from the confidence level.

268
00:17:47,567 --> 00:17:54,467
For 95 percent confidence you look for 0.9750 cumulative left-area,

269
00:17:54,467 --> 00:17:57,833
giving z-star about 1.96.

270
00:17:57,867 --> 00:18:00,700
For a 95 percent two-sided interval,

271
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:03,000
you want 95 percent in the middle,

272
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,033
leaving 2.5 percent in each tail.

273
00:18:06,033 --> 00:18:10,800
Because your z-table gives left area, look for .9750.

274
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:13,867
That gives z-star about 1.96.

275
00:18:13,900 --> 00:18:20,367
Your lecture’s voter example had p-hat around .514 with a large sample and produced

276
00:18:20,367 --> 00:18:27,867
a 95 percent confidence interval roughly from 50.42 percent to 52.38 percent.

277
00:18:27,867 --> 00:18:33,967
Interpretation: we are 95 percent confident that the true population proportion lies

278
00:18:33,967 --> 00:18:35,400
in that interval.

279
00:18:35,467 --> 00:18:41,267
Do NOT say there is a 95 percent probability that the fixed population parameter is

280
00:18:41,267 --> 00:18:42,833
in this particular interval.

281
00:18:42,833 --> 00:18:45,633
The professor explicitly flags that as false.

282
00:18:45,633 --> 00:18:50,667
The formal repeated-sampling meaning is that if we repeatedly took samples and built

283
00:18:50,667 --> 00:18:56,900
intervals this way, about 95 percent of the intervals would capture the true parameter.

284
00:18:58,967 --> 00:19:03,633
Use this when comparing two independent groups on a yes/no outcome.

285
00:19:03,667 --> 00:19:08,467
The point estimate is the difference between the observed percentages in the two

286
00:19:08,467 --> 00:19:10,300
separate soccer groups.

287
00:19:10,333 --> 00:19:13,867
The standard error combines the variation from each group,

288
00:19:13,867 --> 00:19:17,600
with a separate proportion and sample size for each.

289
00:19:18,433 --> 00:19:22,333
Subscripts one and two mean the two independent soccer groups.

290
00:19:22,333 --> 00:19:25,033
Do not reuse one group’s n for the other.

291
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:28,133
Multiply that standard error by z-star,

292
00:19:28,133 --> 00:19:33,200
then add and subtract it from the observed difference in sample proportions.

293
00:19:34,067 --> 00:19:38,433
If the interval contains zero, no population difference is plausible.

294
00:19:38,433 --> 00:19:43,633
If the entire interval is positive, group 1 likely has the larger proportion.

295
00:19:43,633 --> 00:19:49,200
If the entire interval is negative, group 1 likely has the smaller proportion.

296
00:19:51,267 --> 00:19:57,133
Use this when estimating one population average and population sigma is unknown,

297
00:19:57,133 --> 00:20:00,267
which is the normal situation in this class.

298
00:20:00,300 --> 00:20:05,133
Here is the one-mean confidence interval in real mathematical notation.

299
00:20:05,133 --> 00:20:07,733
Read x-bar as the sample average,

300
00:20:07,733 --> 00:20:10,367
s as the sample standard deviation,

301
00:20:10,367 --> 00:20:12,900
and n as the sample size.

302
00:20:13,767 --> 00:20:18,233
The term n minus one underneath t-star is its degrees of freedom.

303
00:20:18,233 --> 00:20:22,300
It does not mean you subtract one from the t critical value.

304
00:20:22,333 --> 00:20:25,100
The standard error is s over square root n.

305
00:20:25,100 --> 00:20:27,867
Degrees of freedom are n minus 1.

306
00:20:27,933 --> 00:20:30,433
This is where the t-table comes in.

307
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:37,633
The t-distribution looks like a Normal bell curve but has heavier tails.

308
00:20:37,633 --> 00:20:42,567
It accounts for the extra uncertainty created because we do not know the population

309
00:20:42,567 --> 00:20:49,067
standard deviation sigma and must estimate it using the sample standard deviation s.

310
00:20:49,133 --> 00:20:53,000
The t-distribution changes depending on degrees of freedom.

311
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:56,000
With small degrees of freedom, the tails are heavier.

312
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,733
As degrees of freedom become very large,

313
00:20:58,733 --> 00:21:02,967
the t-distribution approaches the standard Normal distribution.

314
00:21:03,033 --> 00:21:05,833
Your t-table is different from your z-table.

315
00:21:05,833 --> 00:21:09,233
The z-table gives cumulative area to the LEFT of z.

316
00:21:09,233 --> 00:21:14,567
The t-table gives a CRITICAL t value for a chosen UPPER-TAIL probability and a

317
00:21:14,567 --> 00:21:16,633
chosen degrees of freedom.

318
00:21:16,700 --> 00:21:21,167
So with t, you do not usually search for an exact p-value the same way.

319
00:21:21,167 --> 00:21:26,733
You choose the row for degrees of freedom and the column for the upper-tail area you need.

320
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:30,200
For a 95 percent two-sided confidence interval,

321
00:21:30,200 --> 00:21:35,433
alpha equals .05 total, so each tail has .025.

322
00:21:35,433 --> 00:21:40,933
Go to df equals n minus 1, then the .025 upper-tail column.

323
00:21:40,933 --> 00:21:43,100
That t value is t-star.

324
00:21:43,133 --> 00:21:45,467
For a 90 percent two-sided interval,

325
00:21:45,467 --> 00:21:51,100
each tail is .05, so use the .05 upper-tail column.

326
00:21:51,133 --> 00:21:54,667
For a one-sided 90 percent lower confidence bound,

327
00:21:54,667 --> 00:21:57,567
you keep 90 percent above the lower bound,

328
00:21:57,567 --> 00:22:00,633
so alpha is .10 in the lower tail.

329
00:22:00,633 --> 00:22:06,067
Because the t table is an upper-tail table and the t distribution is symmetric,

330
00:22:06,067 --> 00:22:12,133
you can use the positive t critical value with upper-tail .10 and place it into the

331
00:22:12,133 --> 00:22:14,800
correct lower-bound formula.

332
00:22:15,667 --> 00:22:19,033
One-sided intervals have only one finite endpoint.

333
00:22:19,033 --> 00:22:23,633
A lower confidence bound looks like point estimate minus margin of error,

334
00:22:23,633 --> 00:22:24,933
up to infinity.

335
00:22:24,933 --> 00:22:30,200
An upper confidence bound looks like negative infinity up to point estimate plus

336
00:22:30,200 --> 00:22:31,733
margin of error.

337
00:22:33,767 --> 00:22:38,500
Use when comparing averages from two separate, independent groups.

338
00:22:38,533 --> 00:22:43,733
The point estimate is the difference between two independent sample averages.

339
00:22:43,800 --> 00:22:48,600
The standard error adds the estimated variance divided by sample size for each

340
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:51,500
group, and then takes the square root.

341
00:22:52,333 --> 00:22:56,000
Use a t critical value with Welch degrees of freedom.

342
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,467
Your professor calls it the “crazy df.”

343
00:22:58,467 --> 00:23:03,500
On the exam, if you need it, use the provided formula or information.

344
00:23:04,333 --> 00:23:09,367
The Greek letter nu, or df, labels the Welch approximate degrees of freedom.

345
00:23:09,367 --> 00:23:11,800
If the table lacks that exact row,

346
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:17,167
use the professor’s specified rounding convention or a conservative lower row.

347
00:23:17,200 --> 00:23:19,500
Again, zero is the key reference.

348
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:23,967
If a confidence interval for mu1 minus mu2 contains zero,

349
00:23:23,967 --> 00:23:27,133
equal population means remain plausible.

350
00:23:29,167 --> 00:23:33,300
Use paired methods when each observation has a natural partner.

351
00:23:33,300 --> 00:23:38,800
Soccer example: each goalkeeper completes the same reflex drill before training and

352
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:40,233
again after training.

353
00:23:40,233 --> 00:23:43,333
Compute each player’s after-minus-before difference.

354
00:23:43,333 --> 00:23:48,367
Another example is taking both left-foot and right-foot passing accuracy

355
00:23:48,367 --> 00:23:50,600
measurements on the same players.

356
00:23:50,667 --> 00:23:53,533
Do not treat these as two independent samples.

357
00:23:53,533 --> 00:23:55,733
First compute each pair’s difference d.

358
00:23:55,733 --> 00:23:58,433
Then you have one sample of differences.

359
00:23:58,467 --> 00:24:01,067
Here is the paired-data confidence interval.

360
00:24:01,067 --> 00:24:05,000
D-bar is the average of the player-by-player differences;

361
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:09,200
s-sub-d is the standard deviation of those differences;

362
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:13,767
n is the number of pairs, not the number of individual measurements.

363
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:19,633
If a problem says “same individuals before and after,” think PAIRED immediately.

364
00:24:22,167 --> 00:24:26,600
The professor’s guide specifically says you should be able to calculate the sample

365
00:24:26,600 --> 00:24:31,233
size needed for a desired margin of error for proportions.

366
00:24:31,300 --> 00:24:35,433
Begin with the margin-of-error formula for a single proportion,

367
00:24:35,433 --> 00:24:39,700
then algebraically rearrange it to solve for the sample size.

368
00:24:40,533 --> 00:24:42,000
Solve for n:

369
00:24:42,067 --> 00:24:44,700
Here is that solved sample-size equation.

370
00:24:44,700 --> 00:24:47,700
Remember that E is your target margin of error,

371
00:24:47,700 --> 00:24:51,400
expressed as a proportion rather than a percentage.

372
00:24:52,233 --> 00:24:55,367
If a prior estimate for p-hat is given, use it.

373
00:24:55,367 --> 00:25:01,300
If no estimate is given, use p-hat equals .5 and q-hat equals .5.

374
00:25:01,300 --> 00:25:02,000
Why?

375
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:05,767
Because .5 times .5 equals .25,

376
00:25:05,767 --> 00:25:08,200
the largest possible p times q,

377
00:25:08,200 --> 00:25:12,567
so it gives the safest, largest required sample size.

378
00:25:12,633 --> 00:25:15,467
Always round required sample size UP,

379
00:25:15,467 --> 00:25:20,367
because a smaller sample would fail to guarantee the desired margin of error.

380
00:25:20,433 --> 00:25:23,267
Example: 95 percent confidence,

381
00:25:23,267 --> 00:25:32,733
margin of error .01, no prior estimate. z-star is 1.96. n equals 1.96 squared times

382
00:25:32,733 --> 00:25:36,267
.25 divided by .01 squared.

383
00:25:36,267 --> 00:25:38,933
That is about 9,604.

384
00:25:38,933 --> 00:25:40,633
Round up if needed.

385
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:48,367
Confidence intervals ask, “What parameter values are plausible?”

386
00:25:48,400 --> 00:25:54,767
Hypothesis tests ask, “Is this specific claim about the parameter compatible with the sample?”

387
00:25:54,800 --> 00:25:58,233
Every hypothesis test has two hypotheses.

388
00:25:58,267 --> 00:26:03,733
The null hypothesis, H-zero, is the default claim you assume for the calculation.

389
00:26:03,733 --> 00:26:06,200
It normally contains equality.

390
00:26:06,233 --> 00:26:11,100
The alternative hypothesis, H-a, is what you are trying to find evidence for.

391
00:26:11,100 --> 00:26:14,300
It can be less than, greater than, or not equal.

392
00:26:14,333 --> 00:26:18,567
The alternative must be chosen before looking at the sample result.

393
00:26:18,567 --> 00:26:24,300
You cannot look at the data and then choose whichever direction makes the p-value smaller.

394
00:26:24,333 --> 00:26:25,900
The workflow is:

395
00:26:25,967 --> 00:26:28,233
State H-zero and H-a.

396
00:26:28,267 --> 00:26:29,933
Check conditions.

397
00:26:29,967 --> 00:26:32,333
Calculate the test statistic.

398
00:26:32,400 --> 00:26:36,600
Use the correct distribution and tail to find the p-value.

399
00:26:36,633 --> 00:26:38,867
Compare p-value with alpha.

400
00:26:38,900 --> 00:26:41,333
Write the conclusion in context.

401
00:26:41,367 --> 00:26:46,233
The p-value is one of the most misunderstood ideas, so let’s slow down.

402
00:26:46,300 --> 00:26:50,400
The p-value is NOT the probability that H-zero is true.

403
00:26:50,467 --> 00:26:52,700
Instead, assume H-zero is true.

404
00:26:52,700 --> 00:26:58,033
Then ask: what is the probability of seeing a sample result this extreme or even

405
00:26:58,033 --> 00:27:02,167
more extreme, in the direction described by H-a?

406
00:27:02,233 --> 00:27:04,033
That is the p-value.

407
00:27:04,067 --> 00:27:08,800
Small p-value means the observed sample would be hard to explain if H-zero were

408
00:27:08,800 --> 00:27:11,800
true, so we reject H-zero.

409
00:27:11,833 --> 00:27:16,667
Large p-value means the sample is reasonably compatible with H-zero,

410
00:27:16,667 --> 00:27:19,300
so we fail to reject H-zero.

411
00:27:19,333 --> 00:27:24,733
Decision rule: if the p-value is less than or equal to alpha, reject H-zero.

412
00:27:24,733 --> 00:27:26,967
Otherwise fail to reject H-zero.

413
00:27:26,967 --> 00:27:30,800
In most exam examples exact equality is unlikely,

414
00:27:30,800 --> 00:27:33,933
but the conventional rule includes equality.

415
00:27:37,267 --> 00:27:41,433
This is a calculation skill you absolutely want automatic.

416
00:27:41,500 --> 00:27:44,667
If H-a says parameter is LESS THAN the null value,

417
00:27:44,667 --> 00:27:47,467
use the area BELOW the test statistic.

418
00:27:47,467 --> 00:27:49,233
Left-tailed test.

419
00:27:50,100 --> 00:27:54,533
If H-a says GREATER THAN, use the area ABOVE the test statistic.

420
00:27:54,533 --> 00:27:56,233
Right-tailed test.

421
00:27:57,067 --> 00:28:00,400
If H-a says NOT EQUAL, use both outside tails.

422
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:02,100
Two-sided test.

423
00:28:02,933 --> 00:28:06,867
For a z-test, because your z-table gives area below:

424
00:28:06,900 --> 00:28:10,333
Left-tail p-value: read the table directly.

425
00:28:10,367 --> 00:28:13,967
Right-tail p-value: one minus the table value.

426
00:28:14,033 --> 00:28:19,333
Two-sided p-value: take the one-tail extreme area and multiply by two.

427
00:28:19,367 --> 00:28:22,033
Keep the professor’s exact voter example:

428
00:28:22,033 --> 00:28:27,933
null p equals 0.52, alternative p less than 0.52,

429
00:28:27,933 --> 00:28:33,233
sample size 10,000, and p-hat equals 0.514.

430
00:28:33,233 --> 00:28:36,567
This is a left-tail one-proportion z-test.

431
00:28:36,633 --> 00:28:39,633
The one-proportion z test statistic is:

432
00:28:39,700 --> 00:28:45,267
The one-proportion test statistic has a different standard error from the confidence interval.

433
00:28:45,267 --> 00:28:51,567
It uses p-zero from the null hypothesis and q-zero equal to one minus p-zero.

434
00:28:52,400 --> 00:28:55,433
P-zero is the proportion asserted under the null.

435
00:28:55,433 --> 00:28:59,600
The numerator compares the observed sample fraction with that claim.

436
00:28:59,600 --> 00:29:05,100
The denominator measures how much normal sample fluctuation the null predicts.

437
00:29:05,167 --> 00:29:08,467
Notice something important: in a hypothesis test,

438
00:29:08,467 --> 00:29:12,633
the standard error uses p-zero from the null, not p-hat.

439
00:29:12,633 --> 00:29:16,733
That is different from a confidence interval, which uses p-hat.

440
00:29:16,800 --> 00:29:20,167
Plugging in gives z about negative 1.2.

441
00:29:20,233 --> 00:29:24,867
Because H-a is less than, use the area below negative 1.2.

442
00:29:24,867 --> 00:29:27,833
The z-table gives .1151.

443
00:29:27,833 --> 00:29:35,833
If alpha is .05, .1151 is larger than .05, so fail to reject H-zero.

444
00:29:35,833 --> 00:29:40,967
There is not enough evidence to claim that true support is below 52 percent.

445
00:29:44,300 --> 00:29:47,433
Again, start by identifying the parameter.

446
00:29:49,467 --> 00:29:52,133
Use for one population percentage.

447
00:29:52,200 --> 00:29:57,400
For one population percentage, use the following one-proportion z-test formula.

448
00:29:57,400 --> 00:30:01,400
The null proportion is p-zero, not the sample p-hat.

449
00:30:02,267 --> 00:30:03,867
Use z-table.

450
00:30:05,933 --> 00:30:09,900
Use to compare two independent population proportions.

451
00:30:09,967 --> 00:30:15,300
Your professor’s original lecture compares female and male students who prefer cats.

452
00:30:15,300 --> 00:30:21,633
The female sample is 13 out of 45, and the male sample is 16 out of 74.

453
00:30:21,633 --> 00:30:25,400
We keep these original numbers as the lecture taught them.

454
00:30:25,433 --> 00:30:28,433
The null says p-F minus p-M equals zero.

455
00:30:28,433 --> 00:30:32,700
The alternative says p-F minus p-M is greater than zero.

456
00:30:32,767 --> 00:30:39,167
The lecture standardizes that sample difference and reports z about 0.878 with a

457
00:30:39,167 --> 00:30:43,267
one-sided p-value about 0.1894.

458
00:30:43,267 --> 00:30:49,267
That is bigger than alpha 0.10, so the lecture concludes insufficient evidence.

459
00:30:49,267 --> 00:30:53,400
Follow the exact method taught in class for this professor example;

460
00:30:53,400 --> 00:30:58,633
a textbook may introduce a pooled-null variant of the two-proportion test.

461
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:02,033
Because this is an instructor example,

462
00:31:02,033 --> 00:31:07,400
the lecture’s unpooled standardization is used for the reported numerical result.

463
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:13,500
Do not silently substitute a pooled-test calculation when reproducing that lecture result.

464
00:31:13,533 --> 00:31:20,800
For exam recognition: two separate groups plus yes/no outcome equals two-proportion z procedure.

465
00:31:22,867 --> 00:31:25,433
Use for one population average.

466
00:31:25,500 --> 00:31:27,133
Test statistic:

467
00:31:27,200 --> 00:31:30,833
This is the exact one-sample mean t-test statistic.

468
00:31:30,833 --> 00:31:33,600
X-bar is the observed sample average;

469
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:40,700
mu-zero is the claimed mean; s divided by square root n is the estimated standard error.

470
00:31:41,567 --> 00:31:47,667
T measures how many estimated standard errors the observed sample mean lies above or

471
00:31:47,667 --> 00:31:49,367
below the null mean.

472
00:31:49,400 --> 00:31:52,967
Use t distribution with df n minus 1.

473
00:31:53,033 --> 00:32:00,533
Professor’s GPA example: H-zero mu equals 4.1 versus H-a mu not equal 4.1.

474
00:32:00,533 --> 00:32:08,933
Sample n equals 114, x-bar equals 4.2317, s equals .3664.

475
00:32:09,767 --> 00:32:12,833
The t statistic is about 3.84.

476
00:32:12,833 --> 00:32:16,933
Because the alternative is not equal, it is a two-sided test.

477
00:32:16,933 --> 00:32:23,267
The p-value is extremely small, less than .001 in the table-based conclusion.

478
00:32:23,267 --> 00:32:26,400
If alpha is .02, reject H-zero.

479
00:32:26,400 --> 00:32:32,367
There is evidence that the population mean high school GPA differs from 4.1.

480
00:32:34,400 --> 00:32:38,267
Use for two separate groups and a numerical response.

481
00:32:38,300 --> 00:32:39,933
Test statistic:

482
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:45,333
The two-independent-means t statistic divides the observed difference minus the

483
00:32:45,333 --> 00:32:49,200
hypothesized difference by the combined standard error.

484
00:32:49,200 --> 00:32:53,267
In the professor’s test the hypothesized difference is zero.

485
00:32:54,133 --> 00:32:59,267
Delta-zero is the hypothesized difference between group means, commonly zero.

486
00:32:59,267 --> 00:33:03,833
The denominator combines the uncertainty from both separate groups.

487
00:33:03,900 --> 00:33:07,400
Use Welch t and the crazy degrees of freedom.

488
00:33:07,433 --> 00:33:17,367
Professor example: first-gen mean GPA about 4.1467 versus non-first-gen mean about 4.2466.

489
00:33:17,367 --> 00:33:21,300
The test statistic is about negative 1.083.

490
00:33:21,300 --> 00:33:24,833
The one-sided p-value is about .145.

491
00:33:24,833 --> 00:33:27,667
At alpha .03, fail to reject.

492
00:33:27,667 --> 00:33:33,633
There is not enough evidence that first-gen students have a lower population mean GPA.

493
00:33:36,467 --> 00:33:39,567
If the same people or units are measured twice,

494
00:33:39,567 --> 00:33:45,267
calculate differences first and perform a one-sample t test on the differences:

495
00:33:45,300 --> 00:33:49,667
For paired data, work with the individual before-after differences.

496
00:33:49,667 --> 00:33:55,433
This is a one-sample t-test on d, rather than a two-independent-samples test.

497
00:33:56,267 --> 00:34:00,000
D-zero is the difference claimed under the null, commonly zero.

498
00:34:00,000 --> 00:34:03,033
Calculate a single player-level difference first,

499
00:34:03,033 --> 00:34:06,033
then its mean and standard deviation.

500
00:34:06,067 --> 00:34:08,133
Usually d-zero is zero.

501
00:34:10,700 --> 00:34:14,267
Your professor explicitly says to check conditions.

502
00:34:14,333 --> 00:34:16,067
For one proportion:

503
00:34:16,100 --> 00:34:17,733
Randomization.

504
00:34:17,800 --> 00:34:19,667
10 percent condition.

505
00:34:19,700 --> 00:34:21,800
Success/failure condition.

506
00:34:21,833 --> 00:34:23,567
For two proportions:

507
00:34:23,600 --> 00:34:25,967
Randomization for both groups.

508
00:34:26,033 --> 00:34:28,200
10 percent condition for both.

509
00:34:28,233 --> 00:34:30,233
Success/failure for both.

510
00:34:30,300 --> 00:34:32,167
Independent samples.

511
00:34:32,233 --> 00:34:33,667
For one mean:

512
00:34:33,700 --> 00:34:35,333
Randomization.

513
00:34:35,400 --> 00:34:37,267
10 percent condition.

514
00:34:37,300 --> 00:34:40,267
Large enough or sufficiently Normal data.

515
00:34:40,333 --> 00:34:42,433
For two independent means:

516
00:34:42,500 --> 00:34:44,433
Randomization for both.

517
00:34:44,467 --> 00:34:46,233
10 percent for both.

518
00:34:46,300 --> 00:34:49,167
Large enough or sufficiently Normal for both.

519
00:34:49,233 --> 00:34:51,100
Independent samples.

520
00:34:51,167 --> 00:34:57,167
Randomization is usually satisfied if the problem tells you it is a random sample.

521
00:34:57,233 --> 00:35:01,733
The 10 percent condition means your sample should not be more than 10 percent of the

522
00:35:01,733 --> 00:35:04,967
population when sampling without replacement.

523
00:35:04,967 --> 00:35:08,667
This helps justify independence among observations.

524
00:35:08,700 --> 00:35:13,833
For proportions, success/failure means enough expected successes and failures.

525
00:35:13,833 --> 00:35:18,367
For a confidence interval, use n times p-hat and n times q-hat,

526
00:35:18,367 --> 00:35:20,200
and both should be at least 10.

527
00:35:20,200 --> 00:35:24,967
For a hypothesis test, use n times p-zero and n times q-zero,

528
00:35:24,967 --> 00:35:27,167
and both should be at least 10.

529
00:35:28,033 --> 00:35:31,433
For means, your professor’s guide gives a practical rule:

530
00:35:31,433 --> 00:35:35,333
if there is some skew, n greater than 30 may be enough;

531
00:35:35,333 --> 00:35:39,333
if there is a lot of skew, you may want n greater than 100.

532
00:35:39,333 --> 00:35:44,600
If the sample is small, look for a roughly Normal shape and no severe outliers.

533
00:35:47,167 --> 00:35:49,700
This is likely multiple-choice material,

534
00:35:49,700 --> 00:35:53,167
but it is easy points if the definitions are clean.

535
00:35:53,233 --> 00:35:57,067
A Type I error means rejecting a true null hypothesis.

536
00:35:57,067 --> 00:35:59,267
Its probability is alpha.

537
00:35:59,333 --> 00:36:04,133
A Type II error means failing to reject a false null hypothesis.

538
00:36:04,133 --> 00:36:06,233
Its probability is beta.

539
00:36:06,300 --> 00:36:08,333
Power equals one minus beta.

540
00:36:08,333 --> 00:36:13,967
Power is the probability that the test correctly detects a false null hypothesis.

541
00:36:14,800 --> 00:36:20,767
Soccer decision analogy: suppose H-zero says a goalkeeper training program causes no

542
00:36:20,767 --> 00:36:21,667
improvement.

543
00:36:21,667 --> 00:36:26,567
A Type I error would be declaring an improvement that does not actually exist.

544
00:36:26,567 --> 00:36:30,000
A Type II error would be missing a real improvement.

545
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:32,500
Those are different kinds of mistakes.

546
00:36:32,533 --> 00:36:34,000
What increases power?

547
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:35,600
Larger sample size.

548
00:36:35,600 --> 00:36:37,100
Larger effect size.

549
00:36:37,100 --> 00:36:41,933
Often a larger alpha, although that also raises Type I error risk.

550
00:36:41,967 --> 00:36:45,967
Effect size means how far the true parameter is from the null value.

551
00:36:45,967 --> 00:36:49,800
It is easier to detect a huge difference than a tiny one.

552
00:36:52,333 --> 00:36:59,400
A two-sided hypothesis test at alpha .05 and a 95 percent two-sided confidence

553
00:36:59,400 --> 00:37:02,600
interval tell closely related stories.

554
00:37:03,433 --> 00:37:06,733
If the null value is outside the confidence interval,

555
00:37:06,733 --> 00:37:10,200
you would reject the corresponding null at that level.

556
00:37:10,233 --> 00:37:14,700
If the null value is inside the interval, you would fail to reject.

557
00:37:14,767 --> 00:37:18,600
For a difference between groups, the null value is usually zero.

558
00:37:18,600 --> 00:37:25,100
So if a confidence interval for p1 minus p2 or mu1 minus mu2 contains zero,

559
00:37:25,100 --> 00:37:27,500
“no difference” is still plausible.

560
00:37:27,533 --> 00:37:29,933
This is a fantastic exam cross-check.

561
00:37:29,933 --> 00:37:34,800
If your CI includes zero but your two-sided test says reject equality at the

562
00:37:34,800 --> 00:37:38,033
matching level, something probably went wrong.

563
00:37:40,567 --> 00:37:45,267
Now I want to compress everything into a sequence you can mentally run during a

564
00:37:45,267 --> 00:37:47,100
calculation question.

565
00:37:47,133 --> 00:37:50,267
Step 1: What is the question asking about?

566
00:37:50,300 --> 00:37:53,333
A probability for a continuous random variable?

567
00:37:53,333 --> 00:37:55,133
Stay in Module 4.

568
00:37:55,167 --> 00:37:58,133
A range estimating a population parameter?

569
00:37:58,133 --> 00:38:00,800
Confidence interval, Module 5.

570
00:38:00,833 --> 00:38:03,567
Evidence for or against a specific claim?

571
00:38:03,567 --> 00:38:06,233
Hypothesis test, Module 6.

572
00:38:06,267 --> 00:38:08,367
Step 2: What parameter?

573
00:38:08,433 --> 00:38:10,600
One proportion: p.

574
00:38:10,633 --> 00:38:14,200
Difference of proportions: p1 minus p2.

575
00:38:14,267 --> 00:38:16,200
One mean: mu.

576
00:38:16,233 --> 00:38:20,033
Difference of independent means: mu1 minus mu2.

577
00:38:20,100 --> 00:38:23,767
Paired before-after data: mean difference mu-d.

578
00:38:23,800 --> 00:38:25,933
Step 3: z or t?

579
00:38:25,967 --> 00:38:27,933
Proportions: z.

580
00:38:27,967 --> 00:38:29,667
Means: t.

581
00:38:29,700 --> 00:38:33,067
Normal raw-value probability with known mu and sigma:

582
00:38:33,067 --> 00:38:36,267
standardize to z and use z-table.

583
00:38:36,300 --> 00:38:40,367
Every two-sided confidence interval follows the same pattern,

584
00:38:40,367 --> 00:38:45,600
with the exact critical value and standard error depending on the parameter.

585
00:38:46,467 --> 00:38:50,267
For a single population proportion, the formula is:

586
00:38:51,100 --> 00:38:55,700
For a difference between two independent proportions, the formula is:

587
00:38:56,533 --> 00:38:59,700
For one population mean, the formula is:

588
00:39:00,567 --> 00:39:04,633
For two independent population means, the formula is:

589
00:39:05,500 --> 00:39:09,500
For a paired before-after mean difference, the formula is:

590
00:39:10,333 --> 00:39:16,633
Step 5: If it is a hypothesis test, write H-zero and H-a before calculating.

591
00:39:16,667 --> 00:39:19,100
Use the alternative to know the tail.

592
00:39:19,133 --> 00:39:20,733
Less than — left.

593
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:22,467
Greater than — right.

594
00:39:22,533 --> 00:39:24,267
Not equal — both.

595
00:39:24,333 --> 00:39:26,667
Step 6: Check conditions.

596
00:39:26,733 --> 00:39:29,800
Step 7: Calculate test statistic.

597
00:39:29,867 --> 00:39:32,400
Step 8: Use the correct table.

598
00:39:32,433 --> 00:39:34,900
Z-table: area to the left.

599
00:39:34,933 --> 00:39:39,467
T-table: upper-tail critical values by degrees of freedom.

600
00:39:39,500 --> 00:39:42,567
Step 9: Compare p-value to alpha.

601
00:39:42,600 --> 00:39:45,567
P less than alpha: reject H-zero.

602
00:39:45,600 --> 00:39:49,100
P greater than alpha: fail to reject H-zero.

603
00:39:49,133 --> 00:39:52,033
Step 10: Write the conclusion in context.

604
00:39:52,033 --> 00:39:53,700
Do not say “accept the null.”

605
00:39:53,700 --> 00:39:55,200
Say “fail to reject.”

606
00:39:55,200 --> 00:39:59,500
Do not say the p-value is the probability H-zero is true.

607
00:40:02,067 --> 00:40:04,100
Let’s make this automatic.

608
00:40:04,133 --> 00:40:07,300
“Probability that a continuous measurement is between…”

609
00:40:07,300 --> 00:40:11,000
— integrate PDF or use CDF difference.

610
00:40:11,000 --> 00:40:14,000
If Normal, standardize and use z-table.

611
00:40:14,067 --> 00:40:17,767
“Equally likely anywhere between a and b” — Uniform.

612
00:40:17,800 --> 00:40:21,267
“Time until the next or first event” — Exponential.

613
00:40:21,300 --> 00:40:24,200
“Bell-shaped, mean and SD given” — Normal.

614
00:40:24,267 --> 00:40:28,800
“Estimate the percentage of all…” — one-proportion confidence interval.

615
00:40:28,833 --> 00:40:32,500
“Estimate difference in percentages between group A and B”

616
00:40:32,500 --> 00:40:35,333
— two-proportion confidence interval.

617
00:40:35,367 --> 00:40:39,067
“Estimate the average of all…” — one-mean t interval.

618
00:40:39,100 --> 00:40:42,433
“Estimate difference in average between two separate groups”

619
00:40:42,433 --> 00:40:45,067
— two-independent-means t interval.

620
00:40:45,100 --> 00:40:48,733
“Before and after on the same people” — paired t method.

621
00:40:48,767 --> 00:40:50,500
“Is the true proportion equal to,

622
00:40:50,500 --> 00:40:52,400
less than, or greater than…” —

623
00:40:52,400 --> 00:40:54,700
one-proportion z test.

624
00:40:54,733 --> 00:40:59,533
“Do these two groups have different proportions?” — two-proportion z test.

625
00:40:59,567 --> 00:41:03,300
“Is the true mean equal to…” — one-sample t test.

626
00:41:03,367 --> 00:41:08,767
“Do these two independent groups have different means?” — two-sample Welch t test.

627
00:41:08,833 --> 00:41:13,433
If the wording asks for a sample size that achieves margin of error E for a

628
00:41:13,433 --> 00:41:17,900
proportion, use the squared z-star formula shown earlier.

629
00:41:17,900 --> 00:41:24,600
Use the conservative 0.5 and 0.5 if no previous estimate exists.

630
00:41:27,933 --> 00:41:34,600
Trap 1: Mixing up p and p-value. p is a population proportion. p-value is evidence

631
00:41:34,600 --> 00:41:36,600
in a hypothesis test.

632
00:41:36,633 --> 00:41:41,767
Trap 2: Using p-hat in the one-proportion hypothesis-test standard error.

633
00:41:41,767 --> 00:41:43,500
The test uses p-zero.

634
00:41:43,500 --> 00:41:46,233
The confidence interval uses p-hat.

635
00:41:46,267 --> 00:41:48,633
Trap 3: Using z for means.

636
00:41:48,633 --> 00:41:53,000
In this course, means generally use t because sigma is unknown.

637
00:41:53,033 --> 00:41:55,667
Trap 4: Reading the z-table backward.

638
00:41:55,667 --> 00:41:57,667
It gives area to the LEFT.

639
00:41:57,733 --> 00:42:00,900
Trap 5: Reading the t-table like the z-table.

640
00:42:00,900 --> 00:42:03,500
It does not give cumulative probabilities.

641
00:42:03,500 --> 00:42:08,867
It gives t critical values for upper-tail probabilities and degrees of freedom.

642
00:42:08,933 --> 00:42:14,000
Trap 6: Forgetting to split alpha in half for a two-sided confidence interval.

643
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:19,300
A 95 percent two-sided interval has .025 in each tail.

644
00:42:19,367 --> 00:42:25,400
Trap 7: Forgetting to double a one-tail p-value for a symmetric two-sided test.

645
00:42:25,467 --> 00:42:29,267
Trap 8: Treating paired data like independent groups.

646
00:42:29,300 --> 00:42:35,033
Trap 9: Saying “there is a 95 percent probability the parameter is in the interval.”

647
00:42:35,033 --> 00:42:37,867
Use “we are 95 percent confident.”

648
00:42:37,933 --> 00:42:40,467
Trap 10: Saying “accept H-zero.”

649
00:42:40,467 --> 00:42:43,800
Correct language is “fail to reject H-zero.”

650
00:42:43,833 --> 00:42:46,100
Trap 11: Ignoring units.

651
00:42:46,100 --> 00:42:50,867
If lambda is per hour and the question gives minutes, convert units first.

652
00:42:50,900 --> 00:42:55,000
Trap 12: Forgetting zero when interpreting a difference interval.

653
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:57,067
Zero means no difference.

654
00:42:59,600 --> 00:43:01,100
Full soccer problem.

655
00:43:01,100 --> 00:43:08,100
A coach claims the mean goalkeeper reaction time in a reflex drill is 0.400 seconds.

656
00:43:08,100 --> 00:43:10,500
You suspect the group is actually SLOWER,

657
00:43:10,500 --> 00:43:14,167
meaning the mean time is greater than 0.400.

658
00:43:14,167 --> 00:43:21,067
You randomly sample 36 goalkeepers and observe an average of 0.424 seconds,

659
00:43:21,067 --> 00:43:26,133
with sample standard deviation 0.060 seconds.

660
00:43:26,133 --> 00:43:29,333
Use significance level 0.05.

661
00:43:29,367 --> 00:43:34,833
First identify the task: this is a hypothesis test because we are evaluating a

662
00:43:34,833 --> 00:43:37,533
specific claimed population mean.

663
00:43:37,567 --> 00:43:42,500
The parameter is a mean, mu, so use Student’s t and the t-table, not z.

664
00:43:42,533 --> 00:43:47,667
The null says the true mean reaction time is 0.400 seconds.

665
00:43:47,667 --> 00:43:53,000
The alternative says the true mean is greater than 0.400 seconds.

666
00:43:53,000 --> 00:43:56,767
Because the alternative is greater than, this is right-tailed.

667
00:43:56,833 --> 00:43:59,767
Conditions: the problem says random sample.

668
00:43:59,767 --> 00:44:04,700
For the 10 percent condition, the population of eligible goalkeepers should be more

669
00:44:04,700 --> 00:44:08,500
than 360 if sampling without replacement.

670
00:44:08,500 --> 00:44:14,400
With n equals 36, the t method may be reasonable if the reaction times are not

671
00:44:14,400 --> 00:44:18,233
heavily skewed or affected by severe outliers.

672
00:44:18,300 --> 00:44:23,867
Use the one-sample t formula, with the observed mean minus the null mean divided by

673
00:44:23,867 --> 00:44:26,233
s over the square root of n.

674
00:44:27,067 --> 00:44:34,400
The numerator is 0.424 minus 0.400, which equals 0.024.

675
00:44:34,400 --> 00:44:42,200
The standard error is 0.060 divided by square root of 36, or 0.010.

676
00:44:42,200 --> 00:44:45,167
Divide and get t equal to 2.4.

677
00:44:46,033 --> 00:44:50,533
Degrees of freedom equal 36 minus one, or 35.

678
00:44:50,600 --> 00:44:52,833
Because the alternative is greater than,

679
00:44:52,833 --> 00:44:56,367
find the RIGHT-tail area above positive 2.4.

680
00:44:56,367 --> 00:44:59,600
On your t-table with 35 degrees of freedom,

681
00:44:59,600 --> 00:45:08,467
2.4 falls between the critical values for upper-tail probabilities 0.025 and 0.01.

682
00:45:08,467 --> 00:45:14,367
Therefore the p-value is between 0.01 and 0.025.

683
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:20,233
Since that entire p-value range is below alpha 0.05, reject the null.

684
00:45:20,233 --> 00:45:26,400
There is enough evidence that the average goalkeeper reaction time exceeds 0.400

685
00:45:26,400 --> 00:45:31,900
seconds in this group, meaning the average performance is slower than the claimed target.

686
00:45:31,933 --> 00:45:34,100
Now see how everything fits together?

687
00:45:34,100 --> 00:45:35,700
Identify parameter.

688
00:45:35,700 --> 00:45:37,000
Pick z or t.

689
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:38,867
Write the correct standard error.

690
00:45:38,867 --> 00:45:40,767
Determine the tail from H-a.

691
00:45:40,767 --> 00:45:42,400
Use the table correctly.

692
00:45:42,400 --> 00:45:43,867
Compare p to alpha.

693
00:45:43,867 --> 00:45:45,567
Conclude in words.

694
00:45:48,100 --> 00:45:49,733
Another soccer problem.

695
00:45:49,733 --> 00:45:55,067
In a random sample of 100 soccer players, 62 prefer morning training.

696
00:45:55,067 --> 00:46:00,900
You want a 95 percent confidence interval for the true percentage of similar players

697
00:46:00,900 --> 00:46:02,933
who prefer morning training.

698
00:46:02,967 --> 00:46:05,167
Parameter is one proportion p.

699
00:46:05,167 --> 00:46:09,433
Point estimate p-hat is .62. q-hat is .38.

700
00:46:09,433 --> 00:46:12,233
Use z because proportions use z.

701
00:46:12,267 --> 00:46:17,733
For 95 percent two-sided confidence, z-star is 1.96.

702
00:46:17,800 --> 00:46:23,533
Standard error is square root of .62 times .38 divided by 100.

703
00:46:23,533 --> 00:46:28,067
Margin of error is 1.96 times that standard error.

704
00:46:28,933 --> 00:46:35,033
The interval is the sample proportion 0.62 plus or minus that margin of error.

705
00:46:35,033 --> 00:46:39,200
Numerically, the margin is about 0.095,

706
00:46:39,200 --> 00:46:45,900
giving a confidence interval of roughly 0.525 to 0.715,

707
00:46:45,900 --> 00:46:50,033
or 52.5 to 71.5 percent.

708
00:46:50,900 --> 00:46:53,933
Before finalizing, check success/failure:

709
00:46:53,933 --> 00:47:01,500
100 times .62 equals 62 and 100 times .38 equals 38, both at least 10.

710
00:47:01,500 --> 00:47:07,200
Randomization and 10 percent should also be satisfied from the problem context.

711
00:47:07,233 --> 00:47:13,233
Interpretation: we are 95 percent confident the true population proportion is

712
00:47:13,233 --> 00:47:15,900
between the two endpoints you calculate.

713
00:47:15,933 --> 00:47:17,833
If instead the question asked,

714
00:47:17,833 --> 00:47:23,400
“Is the true proportion greater than .50?” that becomes a hypothesis test.

715
00:47:23,400 --> 00:47:29,267
Then H-zero p equals .50, H-a p greater than .50,

716
00:47:29,267 --> 00:47:33,967
and the test standard error uses .50 times .50,

717
00:47:33,967 --> 00:47:36,967
not .62 times .38.

718
00:47:37,800 --> 00:47:43,133
That comparison between CI and test is exactly the sort of distinction you should be

719
00:47:43,133 --> 00:47:44,733
able to make quickly.

720
00:47:47,300 --> 00:47:50,767
If you remember nothing else, remember these ten things.

721
00:47:50,833 --> 00:47:54,667
One: continuous probability is AREA under a PDF.

722
00:47:54,667 --> 00:47:57,167
Exact-point probability is zero.

723
00:47:57,233 --> 00:48:02,333
Two: Uniform means flat; Exponential means waiting until first event;

724
00:48:02,333 --> 00:48:04,967
Normal means symmetric bell curve.

725
00:48:05,033 --> 00:48:09,133
Three: z-score means number of standard deviations from the mean.

726
00:48:09,167 --> 00:48:12,233
Four: your z-table gives AREA TO THE LEFT.

727
00:48:12,300 --> 00:48:17,767
Five: confidence interval equals point estimate plus or minus critical value times

728
00:48:17,767 --> 00:48:19,267
standard error.

729
00:48:19,300 --> 00:48:22,500
Six: proportions use z; means use t.

730
00:48:22,533 --> 00:48:27,833
Seven: your t-table uses degrees of freedom and UPPER-TAIL probability.

731
00:48:27,867 --> 00:48:31,100
Eight: hypothesis-test alternative tells you the tail:

732
00:48:31,100 --> 00:48:34,533
less left, greater right, not equal both.

733
00:48:34,567 --> 00:48:40,367
Nine: p-value less than alpha means reject H-zero; otherwise fail to reject.

734
00:48:40,400 --> 00:48:44,867
Ten: always identify the parameter before touching a formula.

735
00:48:44,933 --> 00:48:47,300
If you practice that decision process,

736
00:48:47,300 --> 00:48:50,200
the formulas stop looking like random symbols.

737
00:48:50,200 --> 00:48:53,433
Every formula is just the same basic idea:

738
00:48:53,433 --> 00:48:58,000
take what your sample says, measure its uncertainty with a standard error,

739
00:48:58,000 --> 00:49:01,400
and compare it to what you want to estimate or test.

740
00:49:01,467 --> 00:49:05,400
That is the real structure behind this entire exam.

741
00:49:07,933 --> 00:49:11,667
When you listen again, do not try to memorize every sentence.

742
00:49:11,667 --> 00:49:14,800
On the second pass, focus on the transitions:

743
00:49:14,800 --> 00:49:17,900
probability to inference, proportion to mean,

744
00:49:17,900 --> 00:49:21,600
z to t, confidence interval to hypothesis test,

745
00:49:21,600 --> 00:49:24,267
and independent groups to paired data.

746
00:49:24,333 --> 00:49:30,300
On the third pass, pause before each formula section and try to predict the formula yourself.

747
00:49:30,300 --> 00:49:32,300
Ask: what parameter is this?

748
00:49:32,300 --> 00:49:33,900
What is the point estimate?

749
00:49:33,900 --> 00:49:35,567
What is the standard error?

750
00:49:35,567 --> 00:49:36,667
Which table?

751
00:49:36,667 --> 00:49:38,033
Which tail?

752
00:49:38,100 --> 00:49:40,400
If you can answer those five questions,

753
00:49:40,400 --> 00:49:43,800
you are no longer guessing your way through STAT 251.

754
00:49:43,800 --> 00:49:45,533
You understand the map.

755
00:49:45,567 --> 00:49:47,300
End of podcast.
